===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[blow|Blow]]ing in [[loud]] and [[abrupt]] [[burst]]s.
#: {{syn|en|blusterous|gusty}}
#: ''Currently, there are '''blustery''' winds blowing in Patagonia.''
#* '''1920''', {{w|Clara Ingram Judson}}, ''Mary Jane’s City Home,'' New York: Barse & Hopkins, “Lost—One Doll Cart,” p.{{nbsp}}117,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/maryjanescityho00goocgoog]</sup>
#*: Fortunately, that May morning was bright and sunny; the breeze blew warm from the southland instead of cold and '''blustery''' from the lake, and it was the very best kind of a morning possible for being out of doors.
#* '''1957''', {{w|Bernard Malamud}}, ''{{w|The Assistant (novel)|The Assistant}},'' New York: Farrar, Straus and Cudahy, Chapter{{nbsp}}1, p.{{nbsp}}8,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/assistantnovel00mala]</sup>
#*: He wished fleetingly that he could once more be out in the open, as when he was a boy—never in the house, but the sound of the '''blustery''' wind frightened him.
# Accompanied by strong [[wind]].
#: {{syn|en|blowy|blusterous|breezy|squally|stormy|tempestuous|windy}}
#: ''Today is such a cold '''blustery''' day!''
#* '''1918''', {{w|Willa Cather}}, ''{{w|My Ántonia}},'' Introduction,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/19810/19810-h/19810-h.html]</sup>
#*: {{...}} '''blustery''' winters with little snow, when the whole country is stripped bare and gray as sheet-iron.
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1986
|author={{w|John le Carré}}
|title={{w|A Perfect Spy}}
|section=
|passage= In the small hours of a '''blustery''' October morning in a south Devon coastal town that seemed to have been deserted by its inhabitants, Magnus Pym got out of his elderly country taxicab and, having paid the driver and waited till he had left, struck out across the church square.}}
#* '''1999''', {{w|Colm Tóibín}}, ''{{w|The Blackwater Lightship}},'' New York: Scribner, Chapter , p.{{nbsp}}88,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/blackwaterlights00toib]</sup>
#*: The drizzle became '''blustery''' rain as she approached Curracloe.
# {{lb|en|of a person}} [[pompous|Pompous]] or [[arrogant]], especially in one's speech; given to [[outburst]]s.
#: {{syn|en|blustering|blusterous|swaggering}}
#* '''1858''', {{w|Thomas Carlyle}}, ''{{w|History of Friedrich II of Prussia}},'' London: Chapman and Hall, Volume 1, Book 3, Chapter 12, pp.{{nbsp}}295-296,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/historyfriedric49carlgoog]</sup>
#*: {{w|William, Duke of Jülich-Cleves-Berg|Duke Wilhelm}} {{...}} seems to have been of a headlong, '''blustery''', uncertain disposition; much tossed about in the controversies of his day.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1930|author={{w|Dashiell Hammett}}|title={{w|The Maltese Falcon (novel)|The Maltese Falcon}}|url=https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20161221/html.php|chapter=1|publisher=Knopf|location=New York
|passage=He talks in a rather loud, '''blustery''' way and has a nervous, irritable manner.}}
#* '''1937''', {{w|Lloyd C. Douglas}}, ''Forgive Us Our Trespasses,'' London: Peter Davies, Chapter 16, p.{{nbsp}}290,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.272804]</sup>
#*: Uncle Miles wished only to dodge the issue that had hurled them apart, offering an effusive and '''blustery''' hospitality as an alternative to the air-clearing discussion which the situation so urgently called for.
#* '''1989''', {{w|Shashi Tharoor}}, ''{{w|The Great Indian Novel}},'' New York: Arcade, 2011, Chapter{{nbsp}}22,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=9eD7DQAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: Vayu was a large, strong, '''blustery''' character, full of drive and energy but [[mercurial]] in temperament.

